yze this situation carefully.\n\n**Current Situation:**\n- My hand: 6♣ 6♠ (pocket pair of sixes)\n- Board: 9♥ 2♥ 3♠ (The Flop)\n- My chips: 19,600\n- Pot history: Pre-flop I raised 300, opponent called\n- Current round (Flop): Opponent checked\n\n**Hand Strength Analysis:**\n- I have a low pocket pair (sixes)\n- The board shows 9♥ 2♥ 3♠\n- My pair of sixes is currently third-best pair possible (behind nines and any overpair)\n- There are two hearts on board (flush draw possible)\n- No obvious straight threats yet\n\n**Opponent's Actions:**\n- Pre-flop: Called my 300 raise (showing some strength but not aggressive)\n- Flop: Checked to me (showing weakness or slow-playing)\n\n**Promising Options:**\n\n1. **Check (0):** Safe, allows me to see the Turn for free. However, this shows weakness and gives free cards to flush/straight draws.\n\n2. **Small Bet (~400-600):** A continuation bet to gather information and protect against draws. This represents about half-pot sizing and tests if opponent has anything.\n\n3. **Larger Bet (~800-1000):** More aggressive protection against draws, but risks more chips with a marginal hand.\n\n4. **Fold (-1):** Too weak given I have a pair and opponent showed weakness by checking. Wasteful of my pre-flop investment.\n\n**Risk Assessment:**\n- Two hearts on board is concerning (opponent could have flush draw)\n- Opponent could have A-high, K-high, or a weak pair checking\n- Opponent could have 9x and is slow-playing\n- My pair of sixes beats most air hands but loses to overpairs or trips\n\nGiven that opponent checked (showing weakness) and there are drawing hands possible, I should make a continuation bet to:\n1. Protect my hand against draws\n2. Get value if opponent has weaker hands\n3. Potentially take down the pot immediately\n\nA bet of around 500 (approximately 60% of pot) seems optimal - not too large to overcommit with a marginal hand, but enough to charge draws and get information.\n\nReason: \nI have a pocket pair in a heads-up