rd in heads-up\n\n**Promising Moves:**\n\n1. **Check (0):** Low risk, see the flop cheaply since I'm in the big blind position. However, this is passive and doesn't build the pot with a good hand.\n\n2. **Raise 200 (min-raise):** Shows strength, builds pot with a premium hand, and applies pressure. Good sizing to see how opponent responds without over-committing.\n\n3. **Raise 400-600:** Larger raise to put more pressure and build a bigger pot with our strong hand. Risk is we might fold out worse hands we want action from.\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** Too aggressive pre-flop with K-10, even suited. We're chip leader and this risks our entire stack unnecessarily on a good but not premium hand.\n\n5. **Fold (-1):** Terrible option - K-10 suited is far too strong to fold pre-flop in heads-up.\n\n**Position & Game Context:**\n- This is hand 16/20, so we're near the end\n- I'm currently winning by 3550 chips cumulatively\n- I have 19,950 chips (nearly full stack)\n- I should maintain aggression but avoid unnecessary risks\n\nReason:\nI choose to raise 200 (minimum raise). K-10 suited is strong enough to play aggressively in heads-up, and a raise accomplishes multiple goals: (1) builds the pot with a good hand, (2) gathers information about opponent's hand strength, (3) takes initiative, and (4) doesn't over-commit chips pre-flop. The sizing is conservative enough to maintain chip control while being aggressive with a premium heads-up hand.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
